{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he shall lay his hand.</b> According to the plain sense, this is the custom of people: when they wish to begin and engage in any act, they would prepare themselves to do it by laying a hand upon it." "<b>His hand.</b> And not the hand of his son, his slave, or his agent." "<b>His hand on the head.</b> And not his hand on top of his [the agent's] hand. <b>His hand on the head.</b> And not on the neck." "<b>On the head of the burnt-offering.</b> Rashi explained: to include a obligatory burnt-offering, etc. As is stated in Torat Kohanim: just as a freewill burnt-offering requires laying on of hands, so too an obligatory burnt-offering requires laying on of hands. For it is stated עולה אחת — one burnt-offering of the freewill type and one burnt-offering of the obligatory type, to say that both this and that require laying on of hands. For it is stated עולה אחת — one burnt-offering of cattle and one burnt-offering of flock — this and that require laying on of hands. And specifically a burnt-offering of an individual requires laying on of hands, but a burnt-offering of the community, of women, and of a non-Israelite do not require laying on of hands, and all of them require libations, even that of a non-Israelite, as taught in a beraita: a non-Israelite who sent his burnt-offering, etc." "<b>To make atonement for him.</b> From the language of כופר (ransom), for the sacrifice serves as ransom for the punishment upon him." "<b>Upon it.</b> For its sake — as in ״כי עליך הורגנו כל היום״ (Psalms 44:23), ״כי עליך נשאתי חרפה״ (Psalms 69:8)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:39.924840080Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}